<Note
  track="math"
  slug="functions-exponents-logs"
  lede="Three ideas from school algebra carry most of the math in machine learning: a function is a machine, an exponent is repeated growth, and a logarithm asks “which power?”. This note rebuilds them with the exact meanings you will need, and shows where each one hides inside a loss function and a language model."
  sections={sections}
>
  <h2 id="why">Why this note matters</h2>
  <p>
    Open any machine-learning formula and you will find these three ideas doing the work. The loss that trains a
    language model is a logarithm. The softmax that turns scores into probabilities is built from
    <Math tex="e^x" />. “Perplexity”, the number everyone quotes for language models, is <Math tex="e" /> raised to the
    loss. Learning rates are written as <Math tex="3 \cdot 10^{-4}" /> and tuned on a logarithmic scale. If these
    symbols are comfortable, the rest of the math track is a series of short steps. If they are not, every later note
    feels harder than it is.
  </p>
  <p>Interviewers do not ask “what is a logarithm?”. They ask questions whose answers are built from it:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>“Why is the loss function the <em>negative log</em> of the probability, not just one minus the probability?”</li>
    <li>“What does a perplexity of 10 mean?”</li>
    <li>“Your softmax returns NaN on large inputs. Why, and how do you fix it?”</li>
    <li>“Why do we sum log-probabilities instead of multiplying probabilities?”</li>
    <li>“Why search learning rates on a log scale?”</li>
  </ul>
  <p>By the end of this note you can answer all five. Each is worked out below and then collected in the interview section.</p>
  <Callout kind="note" label="The scope rule">
    <p>
      This note covers exactly the parts of functions, exponents and logarithms that the later notes use. No
      trigonometry, no complex numbers, no solving exponential equations for their own sake. If a rule is here, a
      later note needs it.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="plain">In plain words</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      <strong>A function</strong> is a machine with an input slot and an output slot. Put a number in, a number comes
      out, and the same input always gives the same output. A vending machine is a function from button to snack. A
      machine-learning model is a function too, but one with adjustable knobs on the side, and training is turning the
      knobs until the outputs are right.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>An exponent</strong> is repeated growth. Fold a sheet of paper in half ten times and it is
      <Math tex="2^{10} = 1024" /> sheets thick. Each fold multiplies, it does not add, and that is why exponential
      things get out of hand so fast.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>A logarithm</strong> is the same story told backwards. It answers “how many folds to reach this
      thickness?”. To reach 1024 sheets takes 10 folds, so <Math tex="\log_2 1024 = 10" />. Logarithms shrink huge
      ranges into small ones and turn multiplying into adding. That second trick is why machine learning cannot live
      without them.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>Each everyday idea has a precise name and symbol. You need both: the first to think, the second to speak.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Everyday idea</th><th>Technical term</th><th>Symbol</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>A machine from input to output</td><td><span class="term">function</span></td><td><Math tex="f(x)" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>The knobs on the machine</td><td><span class="term">parameters</span></td><td><Math tex="f(x;\,\theta)" /> or <Math tex="f_\theta(x)" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Feeding one machine’s output into another</td><td><span class="term">composition</span></td><td><Math tex="f(g(x))" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Repeated multiplication</td><td><span class="term">exponent</span> (power)</td><td><Math tex="a^n" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Growth that compounds continuously</td><td>the <span class="term">exponential function</span></td><td><Math tex="e^x" /> or <Math tex="\exp(x)" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>“Which power gives this number?”</td><td><span class="term">logarithm</span></td><td><Math tex="\log_b x" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>The logarithm with base <Math tex="e" /></td><td><span class="term">natural log</span></td><td><Math tex="\ln x" /> (in code, <code>np.log</code>)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>A function that undoes another</td><td><span class="term">inverse</span></td><td><Math tex="\ln(e^x) = x" /></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="functions">Functions: machines with knobs</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      Picture a coffee machine. The input is which button you press; the output is a drink. It also has knobs on the
      side, grind size and water temperature, that change what comes out for the same button. A model is exactly this
      kind of machine: a fixed recipe, inputs you feed it, knobs it has learned.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Figure n={1} caption="A function turns an input into an output. A model is a function with parameters: knobs that training adjusts.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 720 230" role="img" aria-label="Diagram of a function machine with input x, output f(x) and parameter knobs">
      <g class="sketch">
        <path class="stroke" d="M60 120 H200" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <rect class="stroke fill-gold" x="210" y="60" width="240" height="120" rx="10"></rect>
        <path class="stroke" d="M452 120 H592" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <circle class="stroke fill-paper" cx="270" cy="200" r="13"></circle>
        <circle class="stroke fill-paper" cx="330" cy="200" r="13"></circle>
        <path class="stroke" d="M270 200 L278 190"></path>
        <path class="stroke" d="M330 200 L322 190"></path>
      </g>
      <text x="40" y="112" text-anchor="middle">x</text>
      <text class="small" x="40" y="136" text-anchor="middle">input</text>
      <text x="330" y="112" text-anchor="middle">the rule</text>
      <text class="small" x="330" y="140" text-anchor="middle">for example: 2x + 1</text>
      <text x="640" y="112" text-anchor="middle">f(x)</text>
      <text class="small" x="640" y="136" text-anchor="middle">output</text>
      <text class="small label-blue" x="300" y="228" text-anchor="middle">knobs = parameters</text>
      <text class="small label-blue" x="300" y="46" text-anchor="middle">f</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <p>
    A <span class="term">function</span> is a rule that gives exactly one output for each input. We write the rule
    with a name and the input in brackets: <Math tex="f(x) = 2x + 1" /> means “the function called <Math tex="f" />
    takes a number <Math tex="x" />, doubles it and adds one”. The brackets do not mean multiplication; they mean
    “apply the rule to”. So <Math tex="f(3) = 7" /> and <Math tex="f(0) = 1" />.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three things about functions matter later, so meet them now with small numbers.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>1. Parameters are the knobs.</strong> <Math tex="f(x) = 2x + 1" /> has two numbers baked in, the 2 and the
    1. Make them adjustable and write <Math tex="f(x) = wx + b" />. Now <Math tex="w" /> and <Math tex="b" /> are
    <span class="term">parameters</span>: they are part of the machine, not inputs. With <Math tex="w = 2, b = 1" />
    you get the function above; with <Math tex="w = -1, b = 5" /> you get a different one. When the parameters
    matter, we write <Math tex="f(x;\, w, b)" /> or <Math tex="f_\theta(x)" />, where
    <Math tex="\theta" /> (“theta”) is the standard name for “all the parameters together”. Training a model means:
    the inputs are fixed by the data, so search over <Math tex="\theta" /> for the knobs that give the best outputs.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>2. Functions can be chained.</strong> Feed one machine’s output into another. If
    <Math tex="g(x) = x^2" /> and <Math tex="f(x) = 2x + 1" />, then <Math tex="f(g(3))" /> means “first
    <Math tex="g" />, then <Math tex="f" />”: <Math tex="g(3) = 9" />, then <Math tex="f(9) = 19" />. The other order
    gives something else: <Math tex="f(3) = 7" />, then <Math tex="g(7) = 49" />. This is
    <span class="term">composition</span>, and a neural network is nothing but a long composition: layer 1, then layer
    2, then layer 3. The chain rule in week 2 is the tool for differentiating compositions, so make the idea solid
    now.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>3. A function has a picture.</strong> Plot inputs along the bottom and outputs up the side and you get its
    <span class="term">graph</span>. <Math tex="f(x) = 2x + 1" /> is a straight line, which is why it is called
    <span class="term">linear</span>. <Math tex="g(x) = x^2" /> is a U-shaped curve. Most of what follows is about
    recognising a handful of these shapes on sight; <a href="#zoo">the zoo at the end</a> collects them.
  </p>
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="https://www.khanacademy.org/math/algebra/x2f8bb11595b61c86:functions"
    title="Khan Academy: Functions (Algebra 1)"
    meta="If function notation feels rusty, do the first two exercise sets, “Evaluating functions” and “Inputs and outputs of a function”. Twenty minutes."
  />
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “A model is a parameterized function <Math tex="f_\theta" />: it maps inputs to outputs, and <Math tex="\theta" />
      is the set of learnable parameters. Training is a search over <Math tex="\theta" /> that minimizes a loss. A deep
      network is a composition of simpler functions, one per layer.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="exponents">Exponents and their rules</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      A rumour starts with one person. Every hour, each person who knows it tells one new person. After one hour two
      people know, after two hours four, after three hours eight. After 24 hours, more than sixteen million. Nothing
      was added at each step, everything was <em>doubled</em>, and that is the entire difference between growing by
      addition and growing by multiplication.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    An <span class="term">exponent</span> counts repeated multiplication: <Math tex="2^3 = 2 \cdot 2 \cdot 2 = 8" />.
    The 2 is the <span class="term">base</span>, the 3 is the exponent or <span class="term">power</span>. The rumour
    after <Math tex="h" /> hours is <Math tex="2^h" /> people, and <Math tex="2^{24} = 16{,}777{,}216" />.
  </p>
  <p>Six rules cover everything you will meet. Check each with the small numbers next to it rather than memorising it.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Rule</th><th>Check with numbers</th><th>Why it is true</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><Math tex="a^m \cdot a^n = a^{m+n}" /></td>
          <td><Math tex="2^3 \cdot 2^2 = 8 \cdot 4 = 32 = 2^5" /></td>
          <td>Three 2s multiplied, then two more: five 2s in all.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><Math tex="a^m / a^n = a^{m-n}" /></td>
          <td><Math tex="2^5 / 2^2 = 32 / 4 = 8 = 2^3" /></td>
          <td>Two of the five 2s cancel.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><Math tex="(a^m)^n = a^{mn}" /></td>
          <td><Math tex="(2^3)^2 = 8^2 = 64 = 2^6" /></td>
          <td>Three 2s, taken twice over: six 2s.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><Math tex="a^0 = 1" /></td>
          <td><Math tex="2^3 / 2^3 = 1" /> and also <Math tex="= 2^{0}" /></td>
          <td>Follows from the division rule with <Math tex="m = n" />.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><Math tex="a^{-n} = 1 / a^n" /></td>
          <td><Math tex="2^{-3} = 1/8 = 0.125" /></td>
          <td>Keep dividing by 2 past <Math tex="2^0" />: 4, 2, 1, ½, ¼, ⅛.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><Math tex="a^{1/n} = \sqrt[n]{a}" /></td>
          <td><Math tex="16^{1/2} = 4" /> because <Math tex="4 \cdot 4 = 16" /></td>
          <td>Two copies of <Math tex="16^{1/2}" /> must multiply to <Math tex="16^1" />.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The negative-exponent rule is the one that shows up most. Learning rates are written like
    <Math tex="3 \times 10^{-4}" />, which is <Math tex="3 / 10^4 = 3 / 10{,}000 = 0.0003" />. In code that is
    <code>3e-4</code>. Reading these without pausing is a small skill worth having before you tune your first model.
  </p>
  <Figure n={2} caption="Growth curves. All pass through (0, 1) because anything to the power 0 is 1. e^x grows faster than 2^x; e^(−x) is the same curve mirrored, and it is how probabilities shrink.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 720 290" role="img" aria-label="Graphs of 2 to the x, e to the x and e to the minus x">
      <g class="sketch">
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={axes(growth)}></path>
        <path class="stroke" d={curve(growth, (x) => M.pow(2, x))} stroke-width="2"></path>
        <path class="stroke ink-red" d={curve(growth, (x) => M.exp(x))} stroke-width="3"></path>
        <path class="stroke" d={curve(growth, (x) => M.exp(-x))} stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="7 5"></path>
        <circle class="stroke fill-paper" cx={sx(growth, 0)} cy={sy(growth, 1)} r="6"></circle>
      </g>
      <text class="small" x={sx(growth, 0) + 10} y={sy(growth, 1) + 18}>(0, 1)</text>
      <text class="small label-red" x={sx(growth, 2.05) + 4} y={sy(growth, 7.4)}>eˣ</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(growth, 2.6) + 4} y={sy(growth, 6.1)}>2ˣ</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(growth, -2.9)} y={sy(growth, 7.6)}>e⁻ˣ</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(growth, 3) + 6} y={sy(growth, 0) + 5}>x</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(growth, 0) - 8} y={growth.y - 8} text-anchor="end">y</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(growth, 3)} y={sy(growth, 0) + 20} text-anchor="middle">3</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(growth, -3)} y={sy(growth, 0) + 20} text-anchor="middle">−3</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <p>
    Two facts about Fig. 2 are used constantly. First, <Math tex="a^x" /> is <strong>always positive</strong>: no
    matter how negative <Math tex="x" /> gets, <Math tex="e^{-x}" /> only creeps towards zero and never touches or
    crosses it. Second, it is <strong>always increasing</strong>: a bigger <Math tex="x" /> always gives a bigger
    <Math tex="e^x" />, so the order of inputs is preserved in the outputs. Keep both in mind; they are the reason
    softmax is built from <Math tex="e^x" />.
  </p>
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="https://www.khanacademy.org/math/algebra2/x2ec2f6f830c9fb89:exp"
    title="Khan Academy: Exponential models (Algebra 2)"
    meta="Only the exercise sets on exponent properties and on graphing exponentials. Skip the word problems about bank interest."
  />

  <h2 id="e">The number e</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      Put 1 coin in a bank that pays 100% interest a year. Paid once at the end of the year, you have 2 coins. Paid as
      50% twice, you have 1.5 × 1.5 = 2.25. Paid as 1% a hundred times, 2.70. Paid continuously, every instant, the
      total settles at 2.71828… and stops climbing. That limit is <Math tex="e" />. It is the number you get when
      growth compounds as smoothly as possible.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    <Math tex="e \approx 2.71828" /> is a constant like <Math tex="\pi" />. It is the natural base for exponents
    because of one property, which the calculus notes will use on every page: the curve <Math tex="y = e^x" /> has, at
    every point, a <em>slope equal to its own height</em>. At the point where <Math tex="e^x = 5" />, the curve is
    rising at a rate of 5. No other base does this; <Math tex="2^x" /> rises at about 0.69 times its height,
    <Math tex="10^x" /> at about 2.3 times. Using <Math tex="e" /> makes every derivative involving exponentials come
    out without stray constants, which is the only reason mathematicians, and therefore machine learning, prefer it.
  </p>
  <p>
    You will see <Math tex="e^x" /> written as <Math tex="\exp(x)" /> when the exponent is long, for example
    <Math tex="\exp(-(x-\mu)^2 / 2\sigma^2)" /> in the normal distribution. In code it is <code>np.exp(x)</code> and
    <code>torch.exp(x)</code>. A few values to have in your head:
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th><Math tex="x" /></th><th>−3</th><th>−1</th><th>0</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th><th>5</th><th>10</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><Math tex="e^x" /></td><td>0.05</td><td>0.37</td><td>1</td><td>2.72</td><td>7.39</td><td>20.1</td><td>148</td><td>22,026</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Notice how quickly the right side explodes. <Math tex="e^{10}" /> is already twenty-two thousand, and
    <Math tex="e^{710}" /> is larger than the biggest number a 64-bit float can hold. A model’s raw scores can easily be
    in the hundreds, so “<Math tex="e^x" /> overflows” is a real bug you will meet, and
    <a href="#together">the log-sum-exp section</a> shows the standard fix.
  </p>
  <Source
    kind="watch"
    href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=m2MIpDrF7Es"
    title="3Blue1Brown: What’s so special about Euler’s number e?"
    meta="About 14 minutes. The clearest explanation of the slope-equals-height property. You do not need the calculus it mentions yet; watch it for the pictures and rewatch it in week 2."
  />
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="https://betterexplained.com/articles/an-intuitive-guide-to-exponential-functions-e/"
    title="Better Explained: An intuitive guide to exponential functions and e"
    meta="The bank-interest story in full, with pictures. Ten minutes, no calculus."
  />
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “We use <Math tex="e^x" /> in softmax and the normal distribution because it is positive and strictly
      increasing, so it maps any real score to a positive number while preserving order, and because its derivative
      is itself, which keeps gradients clean.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="logs">Logarithms: “which power?”</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      The rumour reaches a thousand people. How many hours did that take? You are asking “2 to the power of
      <em>what</em> is about 1000?”, and since <Math tex="2^{10} = 1024" />, the answer is about 10 hours. That
      question, “which power gets me here?”, is a logarithm. <Math tex="\log_2 1000 \approx 10" />.
    </p>
    <p>
      Logarithms are useful for the same reason a map is: they shrink things. The rumour count runs from 1 to
      sixteen million; the hours run from 0 to 24. Thinking in hours is thinking in logarithms.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    Formally, <Math tex="\log_b x" /> is the number <Math tex="y" /> such that <Math tex="b^y = x" />. The two
    statements say the same thing in two directions:
  </p>
  <Math block tex="b^y = x \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad \log_b x = y" />
  <p>
    So <Math tex="\log_2 8 = 3" /> because <Math tex="2^3 = 8" />, and <Math tex="\log_{10} 1000 = 3" /> because
    <Math tex="10^3 = 1000" />. The <span class="term">base</span> <Math tex="b" /> is which number is being raised to
    the power. Three bases appear in practice:
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Base</th><th>Written</th><th>Where it appears</th><th>In NumPy / PyTorch</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><Math tex="e" /></td><td><Math tex="\ln x" /> or just <Math tex="\log x" /> in ML papers</td><td>Every loss function, log-probabilities, perplexity</td><td><code>np.log</code>, <code>torch.log</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>2</td><td><Math tex="\log_2 x" /></td><td>Entropy in bits, information theory</td><td><code>np.log2</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>10</td><td><Math tex="\log_{10} x" /></td><td>Learning-rate scales, plots, “orders of magnitude”</td><td><code>np.log10</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <Callout kind="pitfall" label="Read this twice">
    <p>
      In machine-learning papers and in NumPy, PyTorch and every ML library, a bare <Math tex="\log" /> means the
      <em>natural</em> log, base <Math tex="e" />. School and calculators often use <Math tex="\log" /> for base 10.
      <code>np.log(100)</code> is 4.605, not 2. The rest of these notes write <Math tex="\log" /> and mean
      <Math tex="\ln" />.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Figure n={3} caption="The natural logarithm. It climbs steeply near zero and flattens out, crosses zero at x = 1, is negative for inputs below 1, and does not exist for zero or negative inputs.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 720 300" role="img" aria-label="Graph of the natural logarithm with labelled points">
      <g class="sketch">
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={axes(lnPanel)}></path>
        <path class="stroke ink-red" d={curve(lnPanel, (x) => M.log(x), 0.005, 8, 240)} stroke-width="3"></path>
        {lnDots.map((d) => <circle class="stroke fill-paper" cx={sx(lnPanel, d.x)} cy={sy(lnPanel, d.y)} r="6" />)}
      </g>
      <text class="small" x={sx(lnPanel, 0.01) + 12} y={sy(lnPanel, -4.6) + 4}>ln 0.01 = −4.6</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(lnPanel, 0.5) + 12} y={sy(lnPanel, -0.69) + 16}>ln 0.5 = −0.69</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(lnPanel, 1) + 12} y={sy(lnPanel, 0) + 18}>ln 1 = 0</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(lnPanel, M.E) + 10} y={sy(lnPanel, 1) - 8}>ln e = 1</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(lnPanel, 7.39) - 10} y={sy(lnPanel, 2) - 10} text-anchor="end">ln 7.39 = 2</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(lnPanel, 8) + 6} y={sy(lnPanel, 0) + 5}>x</text>
      <text class="small label-red" x={sx(lnPanel, 0.02)} y={lnPanel.y + lnPanel.h + 30}>no log of 0 or negatives</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <p>Read the shape of Fig. 3 carefully, because it <em>is</em> the shape of the loss function you will train with:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><Math tex="\log 1 = 0" />, because <Math tex="e^0 = 1" />. A probability of 1 will mean zero loss.</li>
    <li>
      For inputs between 0 and 1 the log is <strong>negative</strong>, because you need a negative power of
      <Math tex="e" /> to get below 1. Probabilities live here, so log-probabilities are always negative numbers.
    </li>
    <li>
      Near zero the curve plunges: <Math tex="\log 0.1 = -2.3" />, <Math tex="\log 0.01 = -4.6" />,
      <Math tex="\log 0.001 = -6.9" />. Each extra zero costs another 2.3. Nothing stops the fall; the log of a
      number heads to minus infinity as the number heads to zero.
    </li>
    <li>
      Above 1 it keeps rising but ever more slowly. Going from 1 to 7.39 raises the log by 2; going from 7.39 to
      54.6 raises it by 2 again. Multiplying the input by the same factor adds the same amount to the output.
    </li>
    <li><Math tex="\log 0" /> and the log of any negative number <strong>do not exist</strong>. In code they come out as <code>-inf</code> and <code>nan</code>, and they are the most common source of a NaN loss.</li>
  </ul>
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="https://betterexplained.com/articles/demystifying-the-natural-logarithm-ln/"
    title="Better Explained: Demystifying the natural logarithm"
    meta="Reads ln x as “how long to grow to x”. Short, and it makes the negative values and the shape obvious."
  />

  <h2 id="log-rules">The log rules you will use</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      Every log rule is an exponent rule read backwards. Multiplying two powers of 2 adds their exponents, so taking
      the log of a product adds the logs. Nothing new is happening; the same six facts are being described from the
      other side.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Rule</th><th>Check with numbers</th><th>Comes from</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><Math tex="\log(ab) = \log a + \log b" /></td>
          <td><Math tex="\log_2(8 \cdot 4) = \log_2 32 = 5 = 3 + 2" /></td>
          <td><Math tex="a^m a^n = a^{m+n}" /></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><Math tex="\log(a/b) = \log a - \log b" /></td>
          <td><Math tex="\log_2(8/4) = 1 = 3 - 2" /></td>
          <td><Math tex="a^m / a^n = a^{m-n}" /></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><Math tex="\log(a^n) = n \log a" /></td>
          <td><Math tex="\log_2(8^2) = \log_2 64 = 6 = 2 \cdot 3" /></td>
          <td><Math tex="(a^m)^n = a^{mn}" /></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><Math tex="\log 1 = 0" /></td>
          <td>any base</td>
          <td><Math tex="a^0 = 1" /></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><Math tex="\log_b b = 1" /></td>
          <td><Math tex="\ln e = 1,\ \log_2 2 = 1" /></td>
          <td><Math tex="b^1 = b" /></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><Math tex="\log_b(b^x) = x" /> and <Math tex="b^{\log_b x} = x" /></td>
          <td><Math tex="\ln(e^5) = 5,\ e^{\ln 7} = 7" /></td>
          <td>log and power undo each other</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><Math tex="\log_b x = \dfrac{\ln x}{\ln b}" /></td>
          <td><Math tex="\log_2 1000 = 6.908 / 0.693 = 9.97" /></td>
          <td>change of base; any base is a constant multiple of any other</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The change-of-base rule has a practical consequence: <em>which</em> base you use changes nothing except a
    constant factor. The place where a function is smallest is the same whether you measure in <Math tex="\ln" />,
    <Math tex="\log_2" /> or <Math tex="\log_{10}" />. This is why a paper can say “log” without caring, and why
    entropy in bits (base 2) and entropy in nats (base <Math tex="e" />) carry the same information.
  </p>
  <p>Two things that look like rules but are <strong>not</strong>:</p>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <Math tex="\log(a + b)" /> is <em>not</em> <Math tex="\log a + \log b" />. Check: <Math tex="\log_2(4 + 4) = 3" />
      but <Math tex="\log_2 4 + \log_2 4 = 4" />. There is no simple rule for the log of a sum; that gap is exactly
      what the log-sum-exp trick below works around.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <Math tex="(\log a)^2" /> is <em>not</em> <Math tex="\log(a^2) = 2\log a" />. The first squares the log; the
      second logs the square.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="https://www.khanacademy.org/math/algebra2/x2ec2f6f830c9fb89:logs"
    title="Khan Academy: Logarithms (Algebra 2)"
    meta="Do the exercise sets “Evaluate logarithms”, “Use the properties of logarithms” and “Change of base”. Thirty minutes, and it covers every rule above with instant feedback."
  />
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “Logarithms turn products into sums and powers into multiples, and any two bases differ only by a constant
      factor, so the choice of base never changes where a function is minimized.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="why-logs">Why machine learning loves logs</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      Imagine grading a weather forecaster. Each day they say “70% chance of rain”, and then it rains or it does not.
      A fair score should reward them when they gave high probability to what happened, and it should be brutal when
      they said “1% chance” and it poured. Over a year you want one total score for all 365 days. The logarithm is the
      tool that gives you exactly this, and it does so for three separate reasons.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    <strong>Reason 1: products become sums.</strong> A model that assigns probabilities to several independent events
    gives the whole sequence a probability equal to the <em>product</em> of the individual ones. Say a model gives
    probabilities 0.9, 0.8 and 0.95 to three correct answers. The probability of getting all three right is
  </p>
  <Math block tex="0.9 \times 0.8 \times 0.95 = 0.684" />
  <p>Take the log of each and add instead:</p>
  <Math block tex="\log 0.9 + \log 0.8 + \log 0.95 = (-0.105) + (-0.223) + (-0.051) = -0.380" />
  <p>
    and <Math tex="e^{-0.380} = 0.684" />, the same answer. For three numbers this is no easier. For a million
    training examples, or for a 500-token sentence where each token has its own probability, a sum is something you
    can compute, average and differentiate, and a product is not. This sum of logs is called the
    <span class="term">log-likelihood</span>, and almost every model is trained by making it as large as possible.
    The <a href="/notes/math/maximum-likelihood/">maximum likelihood note</a> in week 3 builds on exactly this
    calculation.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Reason 2: tiny numbers stay representable.</strong> Multiply a thousand probabilities of 0.001 and the
    true answer is <Math tex="10^{-3000}" />. A 64-bit float cannot hold anything smaller than about
    <Math tex="10^{-308}" />, so the computer returns exactly 0, and every model you compare returns 0 too. This is
    called <span class="term">underflow</span>. In log space the same quantity is <Math tex="1000 \times \log 0.001 = -6908" />,
    an ordinary number. The <a href="#code">code section</a> shows this happening.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Reason 3: confident mistakes are punished without limit.</strong> Flip the sign and you get the loss
    actually used in training, <Math tex="-\log p" />, where <Math tex="p" /> is the probability the model gave to the
    correct answer. Fig. 4 is its graph.
  </p>
  <Figure n={4} caption="The log loss −log p for one example, where p is the probability given to the correct answer. Nearly free when p is near 1; climbs without limit as p nears 0.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 720 300" role="img" aria-label="Graph of negative log p against p with labelled points">
      <g class="sketch">
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={`M${lossPanel.x} ${sy(lossPanel, 0)} H${lossPanel.x + lossPanel.w} M${sx(lossPanel, 0)} ${lossPanel.y} V${lossPanel.y + lossPanel.h}`}></path>
        <path class="stroke ink-red" d={curve(lossPanel, (p) => -M.log(p), 0.004, 1, 240)} stroke-width="3"></path>
        {lossDots.map((d) => <circle class="stroke fill-paper" cx={sx(lossPanel, d.p)} cy={sy(lossPanel, -M.log(d.p))} r="6" />)}
      </g>
      <g class="sketch">
        {lossDots.filter((d) => d.p > 0.5).map((d) => (
          <path class="stroke-soft" d={`M${sx(lossPanel, d.p)} ${sy(lossPanel, -M.log(d.p)) - 6} V${sy(lossPanel, -M.log(d.p)) + d.dy + 4}`} />
        ))}
      </g>
      {lossDots.map((d) => (
        <text class="small" x={sx(lossPanel, d.p) + d.dx} y={sy(lossPanel, -M.log(d.p)) + d.dy} text-anchor={d.anchor}>
          p = {d.p} → {d.label}
        </text>
      ))}
      <text class="small" x={sx(lossPanel, 1) + 6} y={sy(lossPanel, 0) + 5}>p</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(lossPanel, 0)} y={sy(lossPanel, 0) + 20} text-anchor="middle">0</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(lossPanel, 1)} y={sy(lossPanel, 0) + 20} text-anchor="middle">1</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(lossPanel, 0) - 8} y={lossPanel.y - 6} text-anchor="end">−log p</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <p>
    Compare this with the obvious alternative, a loss of <Math tex="1 - p" />. Being 90% sure and right costs 0.1
    either way. But being 99% sure of the <em>wrong</em> answer, so <Math tex="p = 0.01" /> for the right one, costs
    0.99 under <Math tex="1 - p" /> and 4.6 under <Math tex="-\log p" />. Push to <Math tex="p = 0.0001" /> and the
    first loss barely moves to 0.9999 while the second jumps to 9.2. The log loss keeps caring, more and more, exactly
    when the model is most confidently wrong, and that pressure is what trains calibrated probabilities. It is the
    forecaster’s score from the plain-words box: cheap when right, unbounded when arrogantly wrong.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>A fourth, smaller reason: log scales.</strong> When you try learning rates, the useful candidates are
    0.1, 0.01, 0.001, 0.0001: each a factor of 10 apart, not a fixed amount apart. On a log scale those are evenly
    spaced. The same holds for plotting a loss that falls from 1000 to 0.1: on a plain axis the interesting part is
    squashed into an invisible sliver at the bottom; on a log axis every factor of 10 gets equal room. Whenever a
    quantity varies by multiplication, think in logs.
  </p>
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “We train on the log-likelihood rather than the likelihood because the log turns a product over examples into a
      sum, which is numerically stable and easy to differentiate, and it has the same maximizer. Negating it gives the
      cross-entropy loss, <Math tex="-\log p" />, which penalizes confident errors without bound, since
      <Math tex="-\log p \to \infty" /> as <Math tex="p \to 0" />.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="together">e and log together: softmax and log-sum-exp</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      A model ends with a list of raw scores, one per possible answer, and the scores can be any numbers at all:
      negative, huge, tiny. You want to turn them into percentages that are all positive and add up to 100%. The
      recipe is: raise <Math tex="e" /> to each score, which makes everything positive while keeping the order, then
      divide by the total so they sum to 1. That recipe is softmax. The catch is that <Math tex="e" /> to a big score
      overflows, and the fix uses one log rule.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    Raw scores are called <span class="term">logits</span>. For logits <Math tex="z = (2, 1, 0)" /> the
    <span class="term">softmax</span> is
  </p>
  <Math block tex="\text{softmax}(z)_i = \frac{e^{z_i}}{\sum_j e^{z_j}}" />
  <p>For our three logits:</p>
  <Math block tex="\frac{(e^2,\ e^1,\ e^0)}{e^2 + e^1 + e^0} = \frac{(7.39,\ 2.72,\ 1)}{11.11} = (0.665,\ 0.245,\ 0.090)" />
  <p>
    Check the properties: every entry is positive, they add to 1, and the biggest logit got the biggest probability.
    The <a href="/notes/dl/softmax-cross-entropy/">softmax note</a> in the deep learning track does the full story;
    here the point is only to see <Math tex="e^x" /> doing the job Fig. 2 promised.
  </p>
  <p>
    Now the bug. Logits of <Math tex="(1000, 1001, 1002)" /> are perfectly ordinary for an untrained network, but
    <Math tex="e^{1000}" /> is far beyond the largest float, so every term becomes <code>inf</code> and the division
    gives <code>nan</code>. The fix: subtract the largest logit from all of them first. The exponent rule says
    <Math tex="e^{z_i - c} = e^{z_i} / e^c" />, and the <Math tex="e^c" /> appears in both the top and the bottom of
    the softmax fraction, so it cancels. The probabilities are <em>identical</em>; only the intermediate numbers
    change:
  </p>
  <Math block tex="(1000, 1001, 1002) - 1002 = (-2, -1, 0)" />
  <Math block tex="\frac{(e^{-2},\ e^{-1},\ e^{0})}{e^{-2} + e^{-1} + e^{0}} = \frac{(0.135,\ 0.368,\ 1)}{1.503} = (0.090,\ 0.245,\ 0.665)" />
  <p>
    Taking the log of a softmax gives <span class="term">log-softmax</span>, which is what the loss actually needs
    (remember, the loss is <Math tex="-\log p" />). Using the quotient rule:
  </p>
  <Math block tex="\log \text{softmax}(z)_i = z_i - \log \sum_j e^{z_j}" />
  <p>
    The second term, the log of a sum of exponentials, is so common it has a name,
    <span class="term">log-sum-exp</span>, and the same max-subtraction trick makes it safe:
  </p>
  <Math block tex="\log \sum_j e^{z_j} = c + \log \sum_j e^{z_j - c} \qquad \text{with } c = \max_j z_j" />
  <p>
    For our logits: <Math tex="1002 + \log(0.135 + 0.368 + 1) = 1002 + \log 1.503 = 1002 + 0.408 = 1002.408" />.
    No overflow anywhere. Every deep-learning library’s <code>log_softmax</code> and <code>cross_entropy</code> do
    this internally, which is why you are told to pass raw logits to the loss and never your own softmax output.
  </p>
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="https://pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.nn.functional.log_softmax.html"
    title="PyTorch documentation: log_softmax"
    meta="Note the sentence explaining that it is computed in one step for numerical stability. That sentence is this section."
  />
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “Softmax exponentiates logits to make them positive and normalizes them to sum to one. Exponentiating large
      logits overflows, so we subtract the maximum logit first; the shift cancels in the ratio and leaves the output
      unchanged. The same trick, log-sum-exp, is how log-softmax and cross-entropy are computed stably, which is why
      the loss takes raw logits.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="zoo">The functions to recognise on sight</h2>
  <p>
    Most formulas in the rest of these notes are made from six shapes. If you can sketch each from memory and name
    where it appears, you can read the formulas as pictures instead of symbols.
  </p>
  <Figure n={5} caption="Six functions that make up most of machine learning. Each panel shows x from −3 to 3." wide>
    <svg viewBox="0 0 720 360" role="img" aria-label="Six small graphs: a line, a parabola, the exponential, the logarithm, the sigmoid and ReLU">
      {zoo.map((z) => (
        <>
          <g class="sketch">
            <path class="stroke-soft" d={axes(z.panel)} />
            <path class="stroke ink-red" d={curve(z.panel, z.f, z.name.includes('ln') ? 0.02 : -3, 3)} stroke-width="3" />
          </g>
          <text x={z.panel.x + z.panel.w / 2} y={z.panel.y - 14} text-anchor="middle">{z.name}</text>
          <text class="small" x={z.panel.x + z.panel.w / 2} y={z.panel.y + z.panel.h + 24} text-anchor="middle">{z.where}</text>
        </>
      ))}
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Function</th><th>Shape</th><th>Key facts</th><th>Where you will meet it</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><Math tex="wx + b" /></td><td>straight line</td><td>slope <Math tex="w" />, crosses the y-axis at <Math tex="b" /></td><td>linear regression, every neuron before its activation</td></tr>
        <tr><td><Math tex="x^2" /></td><td>U shape</td><td>never negative, minimum at 0, grows fast</td><td>mean squared error, L2 regularization</td></tr>
        <tr><td><Math tex="e^x" /></td><td>hockey stick up</td><td>always positive, increasing, slope equals value</td><td>softmax, the normal distribution</td></tr>
        <tr><td><Math tex="\log x" /></td><td>steep then flat</td><td>only for <Math tex="x > 0" />, zero at 1, negative below 1</td><td>log loss, log-likelihood, perplexity, entropy</td></tr>
        <tr><td><Math tex="\sigma(x) = \dfrac{1}{1 + e^{-x}}" /></td><td>S curve</td><td>squashes any number into (0, 1), <Math tex="\sigma(0) = 0.5" /></td><td>logistic regression, gates, attention masks</td></tr>
        <tr><td><Math tex="\max(0, x)" /></td><td>flat then diagonal</td><td>zero for negatives, identity for positives</td><td>ReLU activation</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Look at the sigmoid formula with fresh eyes now. It is built from Fig. 2: <Math tex="e^{-x}" /> is huge for very
    negative <Math tex="x" />, so <Math tex="1/(1 + \text{huge})" /> is nearly 0; it is nearly 0 for very positive
    <Math tex="x" />, so <Math tex="1/(1 + 0)" /> is nearly 1; and at <Math tex="x = 0" /> it is
    <Math tex="1/(1 + 1) = 0.5" />. The whole S-shape falls out of the exponential’s behaviour at the two ends.
  </p>
  <Source
    kind="try"
    href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator"
    title="Desmos graphing calculator"
    meta="Type each of the six functions, then change the numbers in them and watch the shape move. Ten minutes of this beats an hour of staring at formulas. Try y = 1/(1+e^(−k x)) and slide k."
  />

