  <h2 id="llm">Where this lives in an LLM</h2>
  <p>Every idea on this page is in the last few lines of a language model’s forward pass.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The model’s final layer produces one <strong>logit</strong> per token in its vocabulary, about 50,000 numbers
      for GPT-2. <strong>Softmax</strong>, built from <Math tex="e^x" />, turns them into a probability for each
      possible next token.
    </li>
    <li>
      The training loss is <Math tex="-\log p" /> of the token that actually came next, averaged over every position.
      That is <strong>cross-entropy</strong>, and it is Fig. 4 applied billions of times.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Perplexity</strong> is <Math tex="e^{\text{mean loss}}" />. A loss of 2.3 gives a perplexity of
      <Math tex="e^{2.3} \approx 10" />: on average the model was as uncertain as if it were choosing among 10 equally
      likely tokens. A loss of 0.69 gives perplexity 2, a coin flip. This is why perplexity is reported instead of
      loss: it has a meaning you can picture.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Temperature</strong> divides the logits before the softmax. Dividing by 0.5 doubles every logit, and
      because <Math tex="e^{2z}" /> stretches gaps between scores, the distribution sharpens. Dividing by 2 halves
      them and flattens it. The <a href="/notes/llm-internals/decoding-strategies/">decoding note</a> works the
      numbers.
    </li>
    <li>
      Model APIs return <strong>log-probabilities</strong> (“logprobs”), not probabilities, for the same reasons as
      Reason 1 and 2 above. A logprob of −0.105 means <Math tex="p = e^{-0.105} = 0.9" />.
    </li>
    <li>
      The learning rate of GPT-2’s training, <Math tex="6 \times 10^{-4}" />, with its <strong>cosine schedule</strong>
      plotted on a <strong>log axis</strong>, is the exponent rules and log scales from above.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="interview">The full interview answer</h2>
  <p>
    The most common form this note takes in an interview is “why is cross-entropy a log?” or “why not just use one
    minus the probability?”. Here is a complete answer in about sixty seconds, built from the short lines above.
  </p>
  <Callout kind="interview">
    <p>
      “The model outputs a probability for the correct class, and we want a loss that is small when that probability
      is high. We use the negative log of it for three reasons. First, likelihoods of independent examples multiply,
      and the log turns that product into a sum, which we can average and differentiate, and which has the same
      optimum. Second, the product of many probabilities underflows to zero in floating point, while the sum of logs
      stays representable. Third, the shape is right: negative log p is nearly zero when p is near one, but it grows
      without bound as p goes to zero, so a model that is confidently wrong is penalized heavily, which pushes it
      towards calibrated probabilities. A loss like one minus p would cap the penalty at one and give weak gradients
      exactly when the model is most wrong.
    </p>
    <p>
      In practice we compute it from the raw logits with log-softmax, subtracting the maximum logit before
      exponentiating so that nothing overflows; the shift cancels in the softmax ratio. And the exponential of the
      mean loss is the perplexity, which is the effective number of choices the model is hesitating between.”
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p><strong>If they push further, be ready for:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>“Why base <Math tex="e" /> rather than 2?” Any base differs by a constant factor, so the minimizer is the same; <Math tex="e" /> makes derivatives clean. Base 2 gives the loss in bits.</li>
    <li>“What is a perplexity of 1?” Loss 0: the model assigned probability 1 to every correct token. Perfect, and in practice a sign of a bug or data leakage.</li>
    <li>“Why does my loss show NaN?” Usually <Math tex="\log 0" /> somewhere: a probability that reached exactly zero, or an overflow in a hand-written softmax. Pass logits to the library loss.</li>
    <li>“Why are logprobs negative?” Probabilities are at most 1 and <Math tex="\log 1 = 0" />, so every log-probability is zero or below.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="questions">Practice questions</h2>
  <p>Answer each one out loud before opening it.</p>
  <QA q="What does f(x; θ) mean, and what changes during training?">
    <p>
      It is a function of the input <Math tex="x" /> whose behaviour depends on parameters <Math tex="\theta" />. The
      input comes from the data and is fixed; training changes <Math tex="\theta" /> to minimize a loss. In a neural
      network <Math tex="\theta" /> is every weight and bias.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Why is e^x used in softmax instead of, say, x² or |x| to make scores positive?">
    <p>
      <Math tex="e^x" /> is strictly increasing, so a larger logit always gets a larger probability; <Math tex="x^2" />
      and <Math tex="|x|" /> would give the logits −5 and 5 the same probability. It is also smooth with a derivative
      equal to itself, which makes the gradient of softmax and cross-entropy simple (prediction minus target).
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Without a calculator: log₂ 32, log₁₀ 0.001, ln(e⁵), e^(ln 7), ln 1.">
    <p>
      5 (since <Math tex="2^5 = 32" />); −3 (since <Math tex="10^{-3} = 0.001" />); 5; 7; 0.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="A model gives the correct answer probability 0.01. What is its loss? And at 0.99?">
    <p>
      <Math tex="-\log 0.01 = 4.6" /> and <Math tex="-\log 0.99 = 0.01" />. The confidently wrong case costs about 460
      times more than the confidently right case.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Why do we add log-probabilities instead of multiplying probabilities?">
    <p>
      Because <Math tex="\log(ab) = \log a + \log b" />: the sum of logs is the log of the product, so nothing is
      lost. The sum is numerically safe (no underflow), it is easy to average and differentiate, and maximizing it
      finds the same parameters as maximizing the product.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What does a perplexity of 20 mean?">
    <p>
      The mean cross-entropy loss is <Math tex="\ln 20 \approx 3.0" />, and the model is on average as uncertain as if
      it were choosing uniformly among 20 tokens. Lower is better; 1 is perfect.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Your hand-written softmax returns NaN for logits around 1000. What happened and what is the fix?">
    <p>
      <Math tex="e^{1000}" /> overflows to infinity, and infinity divided by infinity is NaN. Subtract the maximum
      logit from all logits before exponentiating. The factor <Math tex="e^{\max}" /> cancels between numerator and
      denominator, so the output is unchanged and all intermediate values are at most 1.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Is log(a + b) = log a + log b?">
    <p>
      No. There is no simple rule for the log of a sum. <Math tex="\log(ab) = \log a + \log b" /> is the product rule.
      Computing the log of a sum of exponentials stably is exactly what log-sum-exp is for.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Why does changing the base of the log not change which parameters minimize a loss?">
    <p>
      Change of base: <Math tex="\log_b x = \ln x / \ln b" />, so every base is a positive constant times the natural
      log. Multiplying a loss by a positive constant leaves its minimizer where it was; only the reported value
      changes.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Why are learning rates searched on a log scale?">
    <p>
      Because the effect of a learning rate is multiplicative: 0.01 and 0.001 behave very differently, while 0.011
      and 0.010 behave the same. Spacing candidates by a constant factor (evenly spaced in log space) spends the
      budget where behaviour actually changes.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="If g(x) = x² and f(x) = 2x + 1, what are f(g(2)) and g(f(2))?">
    <p>
      <Math tex="f(g(2)) = f(4) = 9" /> and <Math tex="g(f(2)) = g(5) = 25" />. Order matters in composition; a neural
      network is a specific order of compositions.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What is 3e-4 as a decimal, and what does the notation mean?">
    <p>
      0.0003. It means <Math tex="3 \times 10^{-4} = 3 / 10^4" />. Negative exponents are reciprocals.
    </p>
  </QA>

  <h2 id="practice">Practice problems</h2>
  <p>
    Do these on paper first, then check. The hand problems are the kind interviewers ask you to do on a whiteboard;
    the coding ones build the habits you will use every day. Solutions are hidden under each problem.
  </p>
  <h3>By hand</h3>
  <QA q="1. Simplify: 2³ · 2⁴,  (3²)³,  5⁰,  2⁻³,  16^(1/2),  (e²)(e⁻²).">
    <p>
      <Math tex="2^7 = 128" />; <Math tex="3^6 = 729" />; <Math tex="1" />; <Math tex="1/8 = 0.125" />;
      <Math tex="4" />; <Math tex="e^0 = 1" />.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="2. Expand ln(a b² / c) into separate logs.">
    <p>
      <Math tex="\ln a + 2\ln b - \ln c" />. Product rule for the numerator, power rule for <Math tex="b^2" />,
      quotient rule for the division.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="3. Write log₂ x using only natural logs, then evaluate log₂ 1000 given ln 1000 = 6.908 and ln 2 = 0.693.">
    <p>
      <Math tex="\log_2 x = \ln x / \ln 2" />. So <Math tex="\log_2 1000 = 6.908 / 0.693 = 9.97" />, consistent with
      <Math tex="2^{10} = 1024" />.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="4. Three probabilities: 0.5, 0.25, 0.125. Compute their product directly, then as a sum of base-2 logs.">
    <p>
      Product: <Math tex="0.015625 = 2^{-6}" />. Logs: <Math tex="\log_2 0.5 + \log_2 0.25 + \log_2 0.125 = -1 - 2 - 3 = -6" />,
      and <Math tex="2^{-6} = 0.015625" />. Same answer, and the sum is easier.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="5. How many times larger is the loss −ln 0.1 than −ln 0.9?">
    <p>
      <Math tex="-\ln 0.1 = 2.303" /> and <Math tex="-\ln 0.9 = 0.105" />. Ratio about 22. Being 10% confident in the
      right answer costs 22 times more than being 90% confident.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="6. Solve 2ˣ = 1000 approximately, and 10ˣ = 0.0003.">
    <p>
      <Math tex="x = \log_2 1000 \approx 9.97" />. For the second, <Math tex="0.0003 = 3 \times 10^{-4}" />, so
      <Math tex="x = \log_{10} 3 + (-4) = 0.477 - 4 = -3.52" />.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="7. A model’s mean loss is 1.386. What is its perplexity? (ln 4 = 1.386.)">
    <p>
      <Math tex="e^{1.386} = 4" />. The model is choosing among four equally likely options on average.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="8. Compute softmax(2, 1, 0) by hand. (e² = 7.389, e = 2.718.)">
    <p>
      Sum <Math tex="7.389 + 2.718 + 1 = 11.107" />. Probabilities <Math tex="(0.665, 0.245, 0.090)" />. They add to
      1 and keep the order of the logits.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="9. Compute softmax(1000, 1001, 1002) by hand without overflowing anything.">
    <p>
      Subtract 1002: <Math tex="(-2, -1, 0)" />. Exponentials <Math tex="(0.135, 0.368, 1)" />, sum 1.503, so
      <Math tex="(0.090, 0.245, 0.665)" />. Note it is the mirror image of problem 8, because the gaps between the
      logits are the same but reversed.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="10. Compute log-softmax(2, 1, 0) and log-sum-exp(1000, 1001, 1002).">
    <p>
      <Math tex="\log \sum e^{z} = \ln 11.107 = 2.408" />, so log-softmax is <Math tex="(2 - 2.408, 1 - 2.408, 0 - 2.408) = (-0.408, -1.408, -2.408)" />.
      For the second: <Math tex="1002 + \ln 1.503 = 1002 + 0.408 = 1002.408" />.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="11. Sketch, from memory, y = ln x, y = −ln x for 0 < x ≤ 1, and y = eˣ on the same axes. Mark where each crosses an axis.">
    <p>
      <Math tex="\ln x" /> crosses the x-axis at 1 and plunges towards the y-axis. <Math tex="-\ln x" /> is its
      mirror: 0 at <Math tex="x = 1" />, rising steeply towards the y-axis. <Math tex="e^x" /> crosses the y-axis at
      1 and never touches the x-axis. Compare with Figs. 2, 3 and 4.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="12. The sigmoid is σ(x) = 1 / (1 + e⁻ˣ). Evaluate σ(0), σ(4), σ(−4), and show σ(−x) = 1 − σ(x).">
    <p>
      <Math tex="\sigma(0) = 0.5" />; <Math tex="\sigma(4) = 1/(1 + 0.018) = 0.982" />;
      <Math tex="\sigma(-4) = 1/(1 + 54.6) = 0.018" />. For the identity: <Math tex="1 - \sigma(x) = e^{-x}/(1 + e^{-x})" />;
      multiply top and bottom by <Math tex="e^{x}" /> to get <Math tex="1/(e^{x} + 1) = \sigma(-x)" />.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <h3>Code</h3>
  <QA q="13. Write a function that checks all seven log rules from the table numerically for random positive a, b and random n, using np.isclose. Run it a thousand times.">
    <CodeBlock
      code={`import numpy as np

def check_log_rules(trials=1000, seed=0):
    rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)
    for _ in range(trials):
        a, b = rng.uniform(0.01, 100, size=2)
        n = rng.uniform(-3, 3)
        assert np.isclose(np.log(a * b), np.log(a) + np.log(b))
        assert np.isclose(np.log(a / b), np.log(a) - np.log(b))
        assert np.isclose(np.log(a ** n), n * np.log(a))
        assert np.isclose(np.log(1.0), 0.0)
        assert np.isclose(np.log(np.e), 1.0)
        assert np.isclose(np.log(np.exp(n)), n) and np.isclose(np.exp(np.log(a)), a)
        assert np.isclose(np.log2(a), np.log(a) / np.log(2))
    print("all rules hold")

check_log_rules()`}
    />
  </QA>
  <QA q="14. Show underflow: multiply 400 probabilities of 0.1 directly and in log space. At what count does the direct product first become exactly 0.0?">
    <CodeBlock
      code={`import numpy as np

p = 0.1
direct, logspace = 1.0, 0.0
first_zero = None
for k in range(1, 401):
    direct *= p
    logspace += np.log(p)
    if first_zero is None and direct == 0.0:
        first_zero = k
print(first_zero)          # 324: 0.1**324 is below the smallest positive float64 (about 4.9e-324)
print(logspace)            # -921.03...: still a perfectly ordinary number
print(np.exp(logspace))    # 0.0 again, as soon as you leave log space`}
    />
  </QA>
  <QA q="15. Implement softmax and log_softmax with the max-subtraction trick and compare against torch.softmax and torch.log_softmax on random logits in the range [−1000, 1000].">
    <CodeBlock
      code={`import numpy as np
import torch

def softmax(z):
    z = z - z.max(axis=-1, keepdims=True)
    e = np.exp(z)
    return e / e.sum(axis=-1, keepdims=True)

def log_softmax(z):
    z = z - z.max(axis=-1, keepdims=True)
    return z - np.log(np.exp(z).sum(axis=-1, keepdims=True))

z = np.random.default_rng(0).uniform(-1000, 1000, size=(8, 50))
t = torch.tensor(z)
assert np.allclose(softmax(z), torch.softmax(t, dim=-1).numpy())
assert np.allclose(log_softmax(z), torch.log_softmax(t, dim=-1).numpy())
assert np.allclose(softmax(z).sum(axis=-1), 1.0)
print("stable and matching")`}
    />
  </QA>
  <QA q="16. Plot −log p for p from 0.001 to 1, and on the same axes plot 1 − p. Mark p = 0.01 on both. Write one sentence about what you see.">
    <CodeBlock
      code={`import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

p = np.linspace(0.001, 1, 500)
plt.plot(p, -np.log(p), label="-log p")
plt.plot(p, 1 - p, label="1 - p")
plt.scatter([0.01, 0.01], [-np.log(0.01), 0.99], color="red")
plt.xlabel("probability given to the correct answer")
plt.ylabel("loss")
plt.legend()
plt.show()
# At p = 0.01 the two losses are 4.6 and 0.99: the log loss keeps punishing confident mistakes
# while 1 - p has already stopped caring.`}
    />
  </QA>

  <h2 id="mistakes">Common mistakes</h2>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Reading <Math tex="\log" /> as base 10.</strong> In ML it is base <Math tex="e" />. <code>np.log(10)</code>
      is 2.303, and a perplexity is <Math tex="e^{\text{loss}}" />, not <Math tex="10^{\text{loss}}" />.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Splitting a log of a sum.</strong> <Math tex="\log(a + b) \neq \log a + \log b" />. The product rule
      applies to products only.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Taking the log of zero or a negative number.</strong> It is undefined, and in code it silently produces
      <code>-inf</code> or <code>nan</code> that poisons everything downstream. Clamp probabilities away from zero,
      or better, compute losses from logits with log-softmax.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Feeding softmax output into a loss that expects logits.</strong> <code>nn.CrossEntropyLoss</code> and
      <code>F.cross_entropy</code> apply log-softmax themselves. Passing probabilities applies softmax twice and gives
      a model that trains badly without any error message.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Thinking “exponential” just means “fast”.</strong> It means multiplied by a constant factor per step.
      <Math tex="x^{10}" /> is fast but polynomial; <Math tex="1.01^x" /> is slow at first but exponential, and it
      eventually overtakes any polynomial.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Treating <Math tex="f(x)" /> as “<Math tex="f" /> times <Math tex="x" />”.</strong> The brackets mean
      “apply the rule <Math tex="f" /> to <Math tex="x" />”. It sounds trivial until you meet <Math tex="\sigma(Wx + b)" />
      and need to know what is a function and what is a multiplication.
    </p>
  </Callout>

