  <h2 id="revision">One-minute revision</h2>
  <Callout kind="revision">
    <ul>
      <li>Function: a rule, one output per input. <Math tex="f(x;\theta)" />: inputs from data, parameters <Math tex="\theta" /> learned. A network is a composition.</li>
      <li>Exponent rules: add when multiplying, multiply when raising, <Math tex="a^0 = 1" />, <Math tex="a^{-n} = 1/a^n" />. <code>3e-4</code> = 0.0003.</li>
      <li><Math tex="e \approx 2.718" />. <Math tex="e^x" /> is always positive, always increasing, slope equals height. That is why softmax uses it.</li>
      <li>Log = “which power?”. In ML, <Math tex="\log" /> means <Math tex="\ln" />. <Math tex="\log 1 = 0" />; logs of probabilities are negative; <Math tex="\log 0" /> does not exist.</li>
      <li>Rules: <Math tex="\log ab = \log a + \log b" />, <Math tex="\log a^n = n\log a" />, <Math tex="\log_b x = \ln x / \ln b" />. Not a rule: <Math tex="\log(a+b)" />.</li>
      <li>Why logs in ML: products → sums, no underflow, same optimum, and <Math tex="-\log p" /> punishes confident errors without bound.</li>
      <li>Softmax: <Math tex="e^{z_i} / \sum e^{z_j}" />. Subtract the max first; it cancels. Log-sum-exp is the same trick for log-softmax.</li>
      <li>Perplexity = <Math tex="e^{\text{mean loss}}" />: the effective number of choices. Loss 2.3 → 10; loss 0.69 → 2.</li>
      <li>Learning rates and loss plots live on log scales because their effects are multiplicative.</li>
    </ul>
  </Callout>
</Note>
